**OS3, LAB 75 (writer 295):**
- **Page one:** "Throughout the book I am the Narrator..." now sits after the closing italic line, its place in 00. The cards are tighter, so everything fits the 200 mm page.
- **Page two:** I left room at the foot for the invitation, as text. Publisher: the room is the bottom 24.9 mm of the drawing, from 175.1 mm down to 200 mm. That takes the heading and the paragraph at 9 pt sans; at the book's 10 pt serif it needs about 31 mm. If you cannot lay text over a placed page, the invitation goes as text straight after the spread, as the writer says. The address is never in the drawing.
- **Screenshots (security 006):** make_spread.py now asks publishing/edition.py whether each image may be used. It refuses any file the build would refuse and any not cleared by name, before embedding anything. The same five pass.

**PQ2, the full cover read as a buyer would (conductor 092):**
- **The back's blurb is 234 words.** A paperback's back usually carries 150 to 180, and at this size the type is close to the floor. Storyteller, a trim to about 160 would let it breathe. I change no word.
- **No line about the author.** Most paperbacks carry one short "about the author" line on the back. I propose one line in the editor's own words, with no contact detail, for him to write or approve.
- **The front's last line, "RC2 · NOV 2026 · CC BY 4.0"** (CV3, signed). A buyer reads "RC2" as a draft mark. That suits copies printed for "would you buy this?", so I leave it as signed.
- **Fine as it is:**
  - the spine (title and author, 3.6 mm type in a 7.4 mm spine, inside KDP's margins);
  - the barcode area (bottom right, clear, the right size);
  - the series line;
  - the cast line, the nine heads with "the nine agents of the day".
- **When a shop gives its spine width,** the cover is one command at that width.

